# Cleaning up after ourselves

When iterating on a task, earlier steps often leave behind code that is no longer needed once a direction is settled.

## When to offer cleanup

After the developer signals they are happy with the current direction or final version — e.g. "looks good", "ship it", "that's the one", or they move on without further changes — **offer** to clean up leftover iteration artifacts.

Do not start a cleanup pass unprompted unless they accept the offer.

## Cleanup on codeword

Run cleanup pass when the codeword "scrubit" is provided, especially when used as the only word in a message.

## What to look for

- Unused functions, components, hooks, or modules added during exploration
- Commented-out or feature-flagged code paths that were superseded
- Duplicate implementations kept "just in case" (e.g. two menu handlers, two state shapes)
- Dead branches in conditionals (`if (false)`, unreachable `else`, unused enum variants)
- Imports, types, CSS classes, or test helpers only referenced by removed code
- Temporary logging, debug UI, or scaffolding left from intermediate steps
- Linting errors & warnings

## What to keep

- Code that is still reachable and part of the chosen solution
- Intentional fallbacks or compatibility shims the developer asked to retain
- Anything whose removal would change behavior beyond deleting dead paths

## How to offer

Briefly name what looks removable and ask whether to proceed. Example:

> The native context menu path replaced the custom one — want me to remove the old handler and its unused types?

If they say yes, remove only the dead paths; keep the diff focused.
